import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.Ke88N3OB.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Publishing public npm tools","description":"How to prepare a public tool for npm and recover a missed release","frontmatter":{"navigation":{"category":"CI and releases"},"title":"Publishing public npm tools","description":"How to prepare a public tool for npm and recover a missed release","editLink":"https://github.com/filiphsps/pumpkin-plugins/edit/master/docs/npm-publishing.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guides/npm-publishing.md","filePath":"docs/npm-publishing.md","lastUpdated":1792509075000}`),a={name:`guides/npm-publishing.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="publishing-public-npm-tools" tabindex="-1">Publishing public npm tools <a class="header-anchor" href="#publishing-public-npm-tools" aria-label="Permalink to “Publishing public npm tools”">​</a></h1><p>This guide covers the first npm publication and the release path for public tools in <code>tools/</code>. Release automation finds packages whose <code>package.json</code> sets <code>publishConfig.access</code> to <code>public</code> and does not mark them private. Package manifests and packed project templates also determine which tools need to publish first.</p><p>If you are adding a tool, register it first. For example, register the codemod package with <code>pnpm register:tool tools/codemod</code>; replace <code>tools/codemod</code> with the new package&#39;s directory. The <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/ci-and-releases#releases">release guide</a> explains registration and how Release Please prepares its release PRs.</p><h2 id="prepare-a-package-for-its-first-release" tabindex="-1">Prepare a package for its first release <a class="header-anchor" href="#prepare-a-package-for-its-first-release" aria-label="Permalink to “Prepare a package for its first release”">​</a></h2><p>npm needs the package name to exist before you can configure its trusted publisher. Plan the placeholder and first functional release together: the first successful OIDC publish must happen within two days of creating the trusted-publisher relationship.</p><p>Publish one metadata-only <code>0.0.0</code> placeholder for each package. Include only <code>package.json</code> and a README that clearly says the package is not functional. Do not include runtime code, commands, or package dependencies. Publish the tarball you created with the <code>placeholder</code> dist-tag; replace the example path with its actual location:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">npm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> publish</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ./path/to/placeholder.tgz</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --access</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> public</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --tag=placeholder</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Run direct npm commands from outside the repository root, such as <code>/tmp</code>, because the root <code>devEngines</code> setting requires pnpm. After each publish, confirm the exact package name and <code>0.0.0</code> version on the public registry. npm assigns the first version to <code>latest</code> as well, so both <code>latest</code> and <code>placeholder</code> point to <code>0.0.0</code> until the first functional release. The release workflow then moves <code>latest</code> to the functional version and leaves <code>placeholder</code> on <code>0.0.0</code>.</p><p>Once npm shows the placeholder, configure that package&#39;s trusted publisher using <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/npm-trusted-publishing">Trusted publishing for npm tools</a>. Do this immediately after confirming the package. The placeholder only reserves the name; it does not test OIDC. Arrange for the first functional release to publish successfully within two days of setting up trust. npm versions are immutable, so do not try to republish <code>0.0.0</code> with different contents.</p><h2 id="how-functional-releases-reach-npm" tabindex="-1">How functional releases reach npm <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-functional-releases-reach-npm" aria-label="Permalink to “How functional releases reach npm”">​</a></h2><p>Release Please prepares a release PR after the tool has releasable changes and its transitive public production dependencies have functional releases. The first functional version is <code>0.1.0</code>. When the PR merges, <code>ci.yml</code> runs its required checks and calls the reusable release workflow. That workflow publishes the released tools in dependency order, including dependencies declared in packed project templates.</p><p>Before publishing, the workflow checks the release tag, packed files, exact dependency versions, and the integrity of versions already on npm. If a newly published dependency has not reached the registry yet, it retries the lookup for up to about two minutes. The publish uses npm trusted publishing and provenance; GitHub does not need an npm write token. See <a href="/pumpkin-plugins/guides/npm-trusted-publishing">Trusted publishing for npm tools</a> for the required workflow identity, OIDC setup, and validation window.</p><p>Every normal release run also checks earlier non-draft tool releases on the target commit&#39;s first-parent history. If an earlier version is missing from npm, the workflow publishes it from its original release commit, oldest first, before publishing the current tools. This catches up a release when a newer push replaces an older pending CI run. A version lower than the package&#39;s current <code>latest</code> is published with the <code>backfill</code> dist-tag, so <code>latest</code> stays on the newest release. The workflow doesn&#39;t publish prerelease versions.</p><p>Release PR titles use the full <code>@pumpkin-plugins/&lt;name&gt;</code> for scoped tools, but their tags use the Release Please component name (the tool folder name). The <code>create-pumpkin-plugin</code> title remains unscoped.</p><h2 id="recover-a-missed-publish" tabindex="-1">Recover a missed publish <a class="header-anchor" href="#recover-a-missed-publish" aria-label="Permalink to “Recover a missed publish”">​</a></h2><p>The normal workflow checks for missing npm versions on later runs. To recover one immediately without another push, dispatch <code>ci.yml</code> on <code>master</code> with the full commit SHA targeted by the existing release tag. For example, resolve the codemod tag and dispatch that commit:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">release_commit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#c62739;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;$(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">git</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> rev-parse &#39;codemod-v0.1.0^{commit}&#39;)&quot;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">gh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> workflow</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> run</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ci.yml</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --ref</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> master</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -f</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> release_commit=&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$release_commit</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>For another tool, replace <code>codemod-v0.1.0</code> with its release tag. The recovery run reads existing GitHub releases and selects only npm tool releases whose tags point to the requested commit. It packs from that exact source commit and does not create release tags or PRs. Normal push releases remain pinned to the commit built by their own workflow run.</p><h2 id="manual-first-release-fallback" tabindex="-1">Manual first-release fallback <a class="header-anchor" href="#manual-first-release-fallback" aria-label="Permalink to “Manual first-release fallback”">​</a></h2><p>If you must publish the first functional version directly, <code>pnpm npm:bootstrap</code> can publish the validated <code>0.1.0</code> artifact from its exact release tag. For example:</p><div class="language-sh"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">pnpm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> npm:bootstrap</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> tools/codemod</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --tag</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> codemod-v0.1.0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --expected-owner</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> your-npm-username</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --confirm-write-access</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Replace the package path, tag, and <code>your-npm-username</code> with values for the tool you are publishing. Authenticate to npm as that user and enable account-level two-factor authentication first. The command checks the tagged source, packed package, dependencies, registry integrity, and npm account before it publishes. It does not configure trusted publishing or count as the first successful OIDC publish, so the two-day validation window still applies. Use the normal Release Please workflow for functional releases whenever possible.</p>`,22)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};